---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import Landing from "../components/landing.astro";
import { Code } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "About",
    template: "splash",
  }}
>
  <Landing>
    <section>
      <p>
        Wallace was written by Andrew Buchan (<a
          href="https://github.com/andyhasit">andyhasit</a
        > on GitHub) and released in early 2026.
      </p>

      <h3>Goals</h3>
      <p>
        The goal was to create an object-oriented version of React which gets
        rid of hooks - a truly awful pattern that should never have seen the
        light of day. One upshot of the object-oriented approach is that it
        gives you total freedom to modify any run time behaviour, simply by
        overriding methods.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most declarative frameworks rely on hidden engines which heavily
        restrict your freedom, so that seemed like a unique selling point.
      </p>
      <h3>Name</h3>

      <p>
        In Scotland, the word freedom cannot be uttered without conjuring this
        scene:
        <img
          src="/img/braveheart-1.jpg"
          alt="Mel Gibson shouting FREEDOM in Braveheart"
        />
      </p>
      <p>
        So Wallace was really the only name for it. It also happened to be
        available on <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/wallace">npm</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over time the object-oriented approach yielded many other benefits,
        turning Wallace into one of the smallest, fastest, nicest and most
        powerful frameworks around.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the name may not be as fitting as it once was, but at least it
        doesn't suggest it's reactive when it is famously not ¯\_(ツ)_/¯
      </p>

      <h3>Historical note</h3>

      <p>
        There is very little overlap between the real
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Wallace"
          >William Wallace</a
        >
        and his portrayal by Mel Gibson in the movie
        <a href="https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0112573/">Braveheart</a>. However,
        the name Wallace has become synonymous with the struggle for freedom in
        Scotland and abroad.
      </p>
      <p>
        And Mel Gibson's portrayal has been immortalised in Scottish culture,
        mostly for its comedy value, as exemplified in
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HbDnxzrbxn4">this video</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Landing>
</StarlightPage>
